 {"id": "PERF-DUCKBRAIN-001", "title": "[P1] 11% of /api/memories reads take >=3s and the cause is FILE COUNT (20k-29k jsonl files per namespace), not embeddings", "status": "blocked", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-04T01:10:15Z", "reasoning": "Measured against the live daemon 2026-11-04 (read-only, authenticated). Ledger census over 52,139 GET /api/memories calls: 55.7% under 300ms (median 73ms), 20.1% in 1-3s, 8.6% in 3-10s, 2.9% over 10s -> 11.45% at >=3s, and 5,968 of those slow calls carried NO query string, so this is the plain LIST path, not semantic search. My first hypothesis (embedding in the path) was wrong. Direct probes isolate the real variable: namespace 'hermes' (6 jsonl files, 145 rows, 5.6M) answers limit=10 in 32ms and limit=200 in 33ms; 'coding-hermes' (20,054 files, 494,973 rows, 2.3G) answers limit=10 in 1.42-2.00s across three repeats; 'fleet-quality' (29,080 files, 94,483 rows, 346M) took 13.26s cold then 1.82s warm. Latency tracks FILE COUNT, not row count and not payload (fleet-quality returns 2.05MB for limit=200 yet beats a 1.4s limit=10 on file-dense coding-hermes). Two consequences: the tail is a per-read directory-walk/metadata cost that no cache can hide on the first call, and 'limit' buys nothing because selection still has to visit the files.", "acceptance_criteria": "Cut the tail with a mechanism, not a cache. (1) Reproduce: a script that, for each of coding-hermes and fleet-quality, runs limit=10 three times cold and reports median vs the file count — committed so the numbers are re-derivable, not quoted. (2) Fix the read path so a limit=10 list does not depend on total file count: compact/merge the namespace's small jsonl files (there is already squash/compaction machinery — prove which one applies) and/or add a bounded index (e.g. newest-N by mtime/segment ordering) that the list endpoint consults before opening files. (3) Prove the effect with the same script: cold limit=10 on both namespaces under 500ms, and no row lost — compare namespaces prune --dry-run plus per-namespace row counts before/after, and confirm GET /api/memories?namespace=X&limit=200 returns the same first-200 newest rows as before the change. (4) Show the ledger's own p95 for GET /api/memories falling after the fix (or state honestly that the window is too short to move p95 yet and give a sampled measurement instead). Do NOT simply raise caches or add a bigger timeout.", "updated_at": "2026-11-04T16:13:43Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "662b29f55d17e638d0a20330321b51bc441043ac924a892cf6e2b22b6c7010ab"}, "worker_status": "done", "commit_hash": "e623f99e76c54075634a4def3daa1deb1be7f15f", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "GREEN", "worker_summary": "gitreins tier1 PASS, tier2 INCOMPLETE on 3 of 5 outcomes. Landed e623f99e: bounded top-k + arg_max total for the plain list path, filtered paths verbatim, 630 tests pass, limit=200 byte-identical (total 241,901 / 53,937).", "foreman_note": "PROOF, PART 1 (compaction) — done on the COPY /home/kara/.duckbrain-live/namespaces, production untouched. 'duckbrain squash --segments --partition=config/2026-08 --namespace=coding-hermes' took the partition 10,000 segments -> 22 (dry-run) and 10,001 files -> 23 on disk; event/2026-08 went 9,798 -> 44. RECORDS PRESERVED: production config/2026-08 = 113,311 rows across 10,001 files; compacted copy = 113,311 rows across 23 files (ROWS MATCH). LATENCY, same machine, same daemon code, --auth=none so no token can distort the read (script /home/kara/clock/prove-perf001-compaction.sh, port 28701): production (fragmented) limit=10 was 1.42/1.88/2.00s across three runs; compacted copy limit=10 is 0.98s cold then 0.47/0.50s warm, and limit=200 returns 1.04MB in 0.51s. So file-count compaction is worth ~3-4x and is the cheap half. IT IS NOT THE WHOLE FIX: the compacted tree still holds ~300 files across partitions (event/2026-09 = 231, plus 23 + 44 + audit) and a limit=10 read still pays ~0.47s, which is consistent with the endpoint opening every partition of the domain and sorting before it slices — i.e. 'limit' still buys nothing. That is the read-path half of the acceptance criteria and is UNSTARTED. Honest state: mechanism identified, 3-4x proven on a copy, target (cold <500ms) NOT met yet, production NOT compacted (owner-gated: it rewrites live memory files).\n\nPRE-FLIGHT FOUND (before anyone compacts production): S3 state is CENTRAL at namespaces/.s3state (not per-namespace), and the CLI already ships 'duckbrain s3 ghosts' + 's3 clear'. Compaction deletes ~10k local files per partition, so the S3 mirror will hold ghost objects for every segment that disappeared — harmless for reads, but it means (a) a naive 'pull' could restore the fragmented layout and undo the compaction, and (b) storage accounting will overstate the namespace. So the production run MUST be: squash --segments (per partition) -> 'duckbrain s3 status <ns>' -> 'duckbrain s3 ghosts <ns>' to enumerate what disappeared -> 's3 clear' (or an explicit sync) so the mirror matches the compacted tree -> re-run 's3 status' to prove convergence. Also confirmed: namespaces/ is INSIDE the duckbrain git repo but gitignored (.gitignore:13), so compaction creates NO git churn — the risk is entirely in the S3 mirror, not git. This pre-flight is not yet exercised end to end; it is written down so the production step cannot be done half-way.\n\nFLEET CENSUS (2026-11-04): the fragmentation is not two namespaces, it is six, and they are 98.7% of every jsonl file the substrate holds. Total across 291 namespaces = 387,175 jsonl files. Top: scheduler 258,578 | pokemon-global 49,622 | fleet-quality 29,080 | coding-hermes 20,054 | hermes-telemetry 14,800 | relationships 10,010 | skills 1,758 | hermes-memory 798 | chat-archive 605 | task-router 46 | routing 40 | default 28. So the production compaction work list is 6 namespaces (~382k of 387k files), not the 2 measured in part 1. Note relationships (10,010 files) is the PERSONAL memory namespace — the one behind Bane's own assistant memory — so the tail is not only a fleet-throughput issue. scheduler at 258,578 files is 67% of the entire substrate by file count and was not in the original measurement at all. Consequence for the acceptance criteria: scale the proof per namespace (rows preserved, latency before/after) rather than declaring victory on coding-hermes alone, and expect the scheduler namespace to dominate the wall-clock of any compaction run.\n\nWorker: deepseek-v4-pro (attempts 1-2 on gpt-6-luna returned partial/design-only; attempt 3 landed). Bench: scripts/perf/perf001-list-latency.mjs (cold+warm labelled). Verdict: .gitreins/history/2026-11-04/e5650949/verdict.json. First judge run 61c5252d tier1 flaked on src/git/autocommit-supapair.test.ts under load; the same test passes in isolation (3/3) and the rerun's tier1 is PASS.", "blocked_reason": "CODE HALF LANDED, DATA HALF OWNER-GATED: the O(nRows log nRows) dedup sort is gone from the plain list path (cold coding-hermes 508->275ms, warm 355->230ms on the compacted copy), but limit=10 still scales with FILE COUNT because collectJsonlFiles() enumerates every segment (fleet-quality 29,080 files = 5.3s cold / 1.7s warm). The remaining half needs the proven segment compaction run against PRODUCTION namespaces, which is a live-data operation plus a daemon restart — Bane's call per HARDEN-DUCKBRAIN-002. Filed as PERF-DUCKBRAIN-002.", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/duckbrain-PERF-DUCKBRAIN-001", "branch": "wt/PERF-DUCKBRAIN-001"}
 {"id": "SUPA9-PK-FILTER-001", "title": "[P0] GET ignores ?pk=eq.N while PATCH/DELETE require it — the same address means 'one row' to a write verb and 'all rows' to a read", "status": "complete", "priority": "P0", "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-11-04T02:30:55Z", "reasoning": "Found by driving the declared-table REST surface end to end on a scratch daemon 2026-11-04 (/home/kara/clock/prove-supa-surface.sh, port 28801/28802, table 'widgets' with primary key id). PATCH and DELETE address rows with ?pk=eq.<value> and both work ({\"updated\":1}, {\"deleted\":1}); a GET with the SAME ?pk=eq.1 returns EVERY row in the table. It is not a general 'unknown filter is ignored' policy either: ?bogus=eq.9 is correctly refused with 400 VALIDATION_ERROR naming the declared columns, and ?id=eq.1 correctly filters on the real column. So 'pk' is special-cased into an accepted-but-unapplied parameter on the read path. Concretely: with 3 rows present, GET ?pk=eq.1 -> all 3 rows, GET ?id=eq.1 -> the one row. Why it matters: the API teaches callers that pk addresses a row (it does for PATCH/DELETE), so an agent that patches and then verifies with the same URL is told its change either did nothing or hit everything — and a delete verification reads as 'still there' only because the row is genuinely gone. That is the exact class of inconsistency that gets an autonomous caller to retry a write that already succeeded.", "acceptance_criteria": "Make read addressing agree with write addressing, and prove all three cases. (1) GET /api/ns/:ns/tables/:table?pk=eq.<v> returns exactly the row whose declared primary key equals v (or a documented 400 if the table declares no primary — not a silent full scan). (2) A falsifier in tests: with 3 rows, GET ?pk=eq.<one> returns 1 row while GET with no filter returns 3, and GET ?<pkcolumn>=eq.<one> returns the same 1 row (alias must not disagree with the column form). (3) An unknown filter column still refuses with VALIDATION_ERROR naming the declared columns (do not relax that to make the alias work). (4) PATCH and DELETE continue to accept ?pk=eq. and their responses are unchanged — prove with the existing suites plus one end-to-end run of declare -> insert 3 -> GET by pk -> PATCH by pk -> DELETE by pk -> GET by pk returns 0. Update the docs/spec (SUPA-3) to state the addressing rule once, so read and write verbs cannot drift again.", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T13:52:07Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "cb86d95abac1f12b5de7354a2c1d471a4b402b524a98fe14632956511d1322e9", "evidence": [{"run_id":"1e915eb9","ts":"2026-11-04T14:34:15Z","tree_fp":"c6f70146d92398ba31db76c787adef85c35955e549bf7834ea21be4a66890633","artifacts":[".gitreins/history/2026-11-04/1e915eb9/verdict.json"]}]}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": "1c8e4be9", "guard_result": "SKIP", "ci_result": "GREEN", "worker_summary": "GET now honours the pk=eq primary-key alias: shared resolvePkFilter() (tables.ts) used by both write verbs and filterParams() on the read path; non-eq pk 400s, no-pk tables 400 instead of returning every row. impl a6df3394, merge 1c8e4be9. Verified: 6 new regression tests (RED pre-fix 4 failed/2 passed, GREEN at HEAD 6/6), focused 52/52, tsc clean, worker full suite 252 files/2094 tests green, test-counts.json resynced in-commit.", "foreman_note": "PRIORITY RAISED TO P0 by Bane 2026-11-04. Rationale: it is the only KNOWN live inconsistency in the Supabase-shaped REST surface, and it corrupts the thing DuckBrain sells — an agent building a feature directly on the database cannot trust that a read address means the same rows as a write address. Fix before the next release cut and before any comparison work is published."}
 {"id": "PERF-DUCKBRAIN-002", "title": "[P1] Compact the PRODUCTION namespaces (fleet-quality 29,080 files, coding-hermes 20,054) — the file-scan half of the /api/memories latency tail", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-11-04T16:13:51Z", "reasoning": "PERF-DUCKBRAIN-001 landed the read-path half (commit e623f99e: cold coding-hermes 508->275ms, warm 355->230ms on a COMPACTED copy) but tier2 judged INCOMPLETE because limit=10 still scales with file count: fleet-quality on the copy is 5.3s cold / 1.7s warm at 29,080 files. The foreman already PROVED the data half on a copy — 'duckbrain squash --segments --partition=config/2026-08 --namespace=coding-hermes' took 10,001 files -> 23 with identical rows (113,311) and cut latency 3-4x. This row is the production execution of that proven mechanism plus the daemon restart that ships the code half. Both are owner-gated: the live daemon's data dir sits INSIDE its git checkout (HARDEN-DUCKBRAIN-002) and restarting :3000 is Bane's call.", "acceptance_criteria": "Owner approval first (this mutates live memory and restarts :3000). Then: (1) snapshot the namespace (git tag + s3 backup prefix) before touching it; (2) run segment compaction per partition for fleet-quality and the remaining coding-hermes partitions, recording files-before/after and rows-before/after per partition — rows must be IDENTICAL; (3) restart the daemon on the merged code (>= e623f99e) and prove GET /api/memories?namespace=X&limit=10 cold under 500ms for both namespaces with scripts/perf/perf001-list-latency.mjs against the LIVE port, plus /health healthy; (4) prove no rows lost on the live surface: total unchanged (241,901 / 53,937 as of 2026-11-04) and first-200 newest rows identical to the saved pre-change responses; (5) add the 500ms assertion + non-zero exit to the bench script (tier2 faulted the current script for logging only); (6) record the ledger p95 for GET /api/memories after a real observation window, or state the window is too short.", "updated_at": "2026-11-04T16:14:35Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "370762bc8b1e24b8ecb3c19d4abae8c6a96edb9ea3d844079b3a815966ea25d6"}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": "SCOPE IS SIX NAMESPACES, NOT TWO (census recorded on PERF-DUCKBRAIN-001, 2026-11-04): 291 namespaces hold 387,175 jsonl files total — scheduler 258,578 | pokemon-global 49,622 | fleet-quality 29,080 | coding-hermes 20,054 | hermes-telemetry 14,800 | relationships 10,010 = 98.7% of every jsonl file. relationships is Bane's personal assistant memory; scheduler alone is 67% of the substrate by file count and will dominate wall-clock. S3 PRE-FLIGHT (mandatory before any production squash): S3 state is CENTRAL at namespaces/.s3state; compaction deletes ~10k local files per partition so the mirror keeps ghost objects, and a naive pull would restore the fragmented layout. Sequence: squash --segments per partition -> duckbrain s3 status <ns> -> duckbrain s3 ghosts <ns> -> s3 clear (or explicit sync) -> re-run s3 status to prove convergence. namespaces/ is gitignored (.gitignore:13) so compaction creates no git churn; the risk is entirely the S3 mirror. Add the 500ms assertion + non-zero exit to scripts/perf/perf001-list-latency.mjs (tier2 faulted the current script for logging only)."}
